What does HIRARC cover?
HIRARC stands for Hazard Identification, Risk Assessment and Risk Control. A hazard is a source or situation with the potential to cause harm. Risk assessment considers how harm could occur and how serious it could be. Risk control addresses what must change to eliminate or reduce that exposure.

Malaysia’s DOSH HIRARC Guidelines, 2008 describe a process of classifying activities, identifying hazards, assessing risks and deciding controls. Worker consultation is an important part of that process.

The legal context also matters. Section 18B of the Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994, updated text as at 1 June 2024 places risk-assessment duties on employers, self-employed persons and principals, and requires implementation of controls where the assessment indicates they are needed. Software can support the records and workflow; responsibility for assessing and controlling workplace risks remains with the relevant duty holders.

What changes when HIRARC becomes digital?
The most useful change is traceability. A supervisor should be able to move from an identified hazard to its assessment, action owner, supporting evidence and latest review without searching through separate files.

Practical question	A connected digital workflow should make visible
Which assessment applies here?	The task, location, equipment and current approved version
Why was this rating selected?	The assessor’s reasoning and supporting evidence
Has the control been installed?	Completion evidence and a named verifier
Does the control work?	Follow-up observations, inspections or other suitable checks
What changed since approval?	New findings, incidents, task changes and review records
Digitizing an old form without addressing these questions can simply move the same gaps onto a screen.

A practical NeoEHS HIRARC workflow
NeoEHS publishes capabilities for configurable risk matrices, action tracking, hazard reporting and digital approvals across its risk and safety modules. The following is a recommended implementation pattern using those capabilities. Approval gates, record relationships and AI functions should be agreed and tested for each deployment.

1. Define the activity before listing hazards
Start with a task that a worker recognises. “Warehouse operations” is too broad for a useful discussion. “Reversing a forklift into the loading bay while pedestrians access the packing area” gives the team something specific to assess.

Record the location, people exposed, equipment, routine or non-routine conditions, and nearby activities. Include operators and maintenance personnel in the discussion. Their knowledge often reveals the workaround that a procedure does not mention.

2. Capture hazards with context
Use NeoEHS Hazard Management to record the condition, location and relevant evidence. Describe the event that could cause harm rather than entering a broad label such as “mechanical hazard.”

For example: “A pedestrian can enter the forklift reversing area through an unprotected opening beside the packing station.” A photograph may support this report, but it will not explain every movement, shift condition or equipment failure mode. The assessor still needs to investigate.

3. Assess risk and keep the reasoning
NeoEHS Risk Management supports configurable matrices and likelihood-and-severity assessment. Agree the rating definitions before users begin scoring.

Keep the rating and the reason together. What exposure was considered? Which controls were already in place? What evidence supports the likelihood selected?

The DOSH guideline’s example matrix calculates risk as Likelihood × Severity, with bands of 1–4 low, 5–12 medium and 15–25 high. Those bands belong to that example method; the organisation should configure and approve the method applicable to its work.

A risk score is a prioritisation tool, not a measured probability that an accident will occur. Nor should a lower total hide a credible fatal consequence or a failed critical control.

4. Turn proposed controls into owned actions
Consider elimination, substitution and engineering measures before relying on administrative measures and PPE. A proposed control needs more detail than “provide training.”

For the loading-bay example, the action might be to redesign pedestrian access and install suitable physical separation. Specify the responsible person, completion date, interim arrangements and verification required. These details make the control actionable.

The workflow should distinguish proposed, implemented and verified effective. A purchase order or a closed task does not, by itself, demonstrate that exposure has been controlled.

5. Review the assessment and authorise the work
Route the assessment through the organisation’s defined review and approval roles. Where work requires a permit, use NeoEHS Permit to Work to support the relevant authorisation, isolation checks and worksite verification.

During implementation, test how the current assessment is referenced in the permit process and what happens if the task changes. An approved HIRARC should never be treated as an unconditional instruction to proceed regardless of conditions.

6. Verify controls in the field
Use NeoEHS Inspection Management to support follow-up checks and evidence collection. Ask whether the control is present, suitable and being used during the actual task.

For a physical barrier, a useful check may include its location, pedestrian access points, condition and how deliveries are handled. “Barrier installed” is a weaker conclusion than a documented check that the separation works during operations.

Only reassess the remaining risk after the relevant controls have been implemented and evaluated. Do not lower a rating simply because an action has been assigned.

7. Review again when the evidence changes
NeoEHS Incident Management supports reporting, investigation and corrective actions. A near miss or repeated finding should prompt the team to examine whether the associated assessment still makes sense.

Recommended review triggers include a changed process, different equipment, new chemical, altered layout, contractor interface, incident or failed control. Define how each trigger reaches the assessment owner and how revised instructions reach affected workers.

Where artificial intelligence adds practical value
AI is most useful when it helps a team find something relevant in a large body of information. NeoEHS describes predictive analytics that brings together safety data to identify patterns and areas needing attention.

For HIRARC, useful questions include: Are several reports describing the same access problem in different words? Have similar maintenance tasks generated repeated isolation findings? Is a hazard recurring after actions were marked complete?

These are investigation leads. They are not proof that a particular event will happen or that a proposed control is sufficient.

For any configured AI-assisted review, good operating practice is to retain the records behind the suggestion, allow a reviewer to accept or reject it, and record the decision. Keep AI recommendations separate from approved assessment content until a responsible person has reviewed them.

Rules and AI also serve different purposes. Calculating a matrix score or sending an overdue reminder can follow a fixed rule. Finding similar narratives across hundreds of reports may benefit from AI. Both can be useful without describing every automated step as intelligence.

Computer vision may add observations for validated use cases, but a camera cannot establish that an assessment is complete. Coverage, lighting, occlusion and non-visible hazards limit what it can reveal.

Worked example: a repeated loading-bay near miss
Consider an illustrative manufacturing site—not a reported NeoEHS customer outcome. Three reports describe pedestrians entering a forklift route. Each report uses different wording, and the existing assessment lists floor markings and high-visibility clothing.

An AI-assisted review could flag the narratives as related, subject to the configured capability and data quality. The safety team then checks the source reports and observes the loading operation.

Stage	Example decision or evidence
Hazard	Pedestrian access into a reversing forklift route
Credible harm	A person being struck or crushed
Existing measures	Floor markings, signs and high-visibility clothing
Assessor’s illustrative rating	Likelihood 4 × Severity 4 = 16
Proposed improvement	Evaluate removal of the crossing and suitable physical segregation
Verification	Observe deliveries and pedestrian access after the change
Reassessment	Record justified residual ratings after verification; retain the potential consequence unless evidence supports changing it
Further review trigger	Barrier damage, route change or another related near miss
The numbers illustrate the method and are not a site assessment. The important decision is whether people and vehicles remain exposed to each other, not whether a dashboard changes colour.

