What should leaders see on a HIRARC dashboard?
Counts of completed assessments are useful, but they say little about whether exposure is controlled. A more useful management review asks about unresolved risk and the quality of verification.

Recommended measures include overdue controls for high-risk activities, critical controls awaiting verification, recurring hazards after closure, and assessments awaiting review following a change. Show both the count and a meaningful denominator—for example, assessments awaiting review out of assessments affected by a recorded process change.

Keep proposed residual ratings separate from ratings supported by completed verification. Show the last field-check date and data gaps. An empty incident history is not evidence that an activity is safe.

Start with one process and prove the workflow
Choose a defined work area or recurring high-risk task. Review the current HIRARC with the people doing the work, agree the matrix and approval roles, then connect findings, actions and verification records.

Test the awkward cases: an overdue control, a rejected AI suggestion, a changed task and a repeat near miss. Confirm that the right person receives the issue and that the assessment can be revised without losing its history.

This gives the organisation a practical basis for expanding digital HIRARC across sites.

